1. Chosen Development Model

Model: Waterfall

Justification: Waterfall was chosen because the project requirements were clear from the start,
the scope is fixed, and the DMAIC methodology follows the same sequential phase-by-phase structure.
Each phase is fully completed before the next one begins — Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, and 
Control — which matches exactly how Waterfall works. The project scope is small and well-defined,
documentation is required at each phase, and there is no need for changes during development.

2. User Requirements

a. Stakeholders

Bank Customers — end-users who book appointments online to avoid queuing and save time.

Bank Staff — employees who serve customers at the counter and manage the daily appointment queue. 
The system includes the following staff roles:
- Teller — handles general customer appointments and basic banking transactions
- Senior Teller — handles more complex transactions and supervises tellers
- Loans Officer — specializes in loan applications and financial consultations
- Supervisor — oversees all staff members and ensures the queue runs smoothly

Admin (Bank Manager) — system administrator who has full control over bookings, staff accounts, 
available services, and support tickets.

Development Team (Amela, Ester, Eriselda) — students who build and maintain the system.


b. User Stories

1. As a customer, I want to book a bank appointment online so that I can avoid waiting in a queue.
2. As a customer, I want to choose a specific date and time slot so that I can plan my visit.
3. As a customer, I want to receive a confirmation code so that I can identify my appointment at the counter.
4. As a customer, I want to view all my bookings so that I can keep track of my appointments.
5. As a customer, I want to cancel a booking so that I can free up my slot if my plans change.
6. As a customer, I want to submit a support ticket so that I can report an issue or ask a question to the admin.
7. As a staff member, I want to see all today's appointments so that I can serve customers efficiently.
8. As a staff member, I want to mark appointments as Completed or No-show so that the system stays updated.
9. As a staff member, I want to search and filter appointments so that I can find specific bookings quickly.
10. As an admin, I want to manage staff accounts and roles so that I can add, remove, or update employees as needed.
11. As an admin, I want to manage banking services so that customers always see accurate options.
12. As an admin, I want to view and manage support tickets so that I can respond to customer issues.
13. As an admin, I want to view booking analytics and export data so that I can monitor system performance.



3. Functional Requirements

a. Description

1. The system must allow customers to register with a name, email, and password.
2. The system must allow customers to log in with valid credentials.
3. The system must allow customers to log out of their account.
4. The system must display all available banking services with name, description, and duration.
5. The system must allow customers to select a service before proceeding with a booking.
6. The system must display a calendar showing available dates for booking.
7. The system must block past dates and weekends from being selected.
8. The system must display available and booked time slots for a selected date.
9. The system must prevent customers from selecting an already booked time slot.
10. The system must allow customers to enter their personal details to complete a booking.
11. The system must validate that all required fields are filled before confirming a booking.
12. The system must generate a unique QB-XXXXX confirmation code for every booking.
13. The system must display a booking summary after confirmation.
14. The system must allow customers to view all their bookings in one page.
15. The system must allow customers to cancel a confirmed booking.
16. The system must allow staff to log in with an Employee ID and password.
17. The system must allow staff to view all appointments in a table with search and filter options.
18. The system must allow staff to mark appointments as Completed or No-show.
19. The system must allow the admin to log in with separate administrator credentials.
20. The system must allow the admin to add and remove staff members.
21. The system must assign a role to each staff member when their account is created.
22. The system must display each staff member's role in the staff management table.
23. The admin must be able to update a staff member's role at any time.
24. The system must allow the admin to add and remove banking services.
25. The system must display booking statistics with a bar chart and a donut chart in the admin dashboard.
26. The system must allow the admin to delete any booking.
27. The system must allow the admin to export all data as a JSON file.
28. The system must allow customers to submit a support ticket with a subject and message.
29. The system must allow the admin to view and manage all submitted support tickets.
30. The system must display the ticket status so the customer knows if their ticket is open or resolved.

b. Acceptance Criteria

FR12 — Confirmation Code Generation
- A unique code starting with QB- is generated after every successful booking
- No two bookings have the same confirmation code
- The code is displayed on the confirmation screen immediately after booking
- The code is visible in the My Bookings page under the booking details

FR8 — Time Slot Display
- Available slots are shown after a date is selected
- Already booked slots are visually different and cannot be clicked
- Slots update correctly when a new date is selected
- All slots are within branch hours 09:00 to 17:00

FR11 — Form Validation
- Customer cannot confirm a booking if any required field is empty
- An error message is shown specifying which fields are missing
- An error message is shown if the email format is invalid
- The booking is only saved after all validations pass

FR17 — Staff Appointment Table
- All appointments are displayed with code, name, service, date, time, and status
- Staff can search by customer name, confirmation code, or service
- Staff can filter by All, Confirmed, Completed, Cancelled, or No-show
- Results update immediately when a search or filter is applied

FR28 — Support Ticket System
- Customer can submit a ticket by entering a subject and a message
- The ticket is saved and immediately visible in the admin panel
- The admin can change the ticket status to Open or Resolved
- The customer can see the current status of their submitted ticket


4. Non-Functional Requirements

a. Description

1. The system must load all pages in under 2 seconds.
2. The booking process must complete in under 5 seconds from start to confirmation.
3. The system must support multiple users performing bookings simultaneously without data conflicts or crashes.
4. The system must respond to any user action in under 1 second.
5. The system must display correctly on desktop, tablet, and mobile screens.
6. The system must save booking data permanently so it is never lost.
7. The system must maintain continuous availability with no downtime during branch operating hours (08:30 to 15:30).
8. The system must preserve booking data integrity if a page is accidentally refreshed during the booking process.
9. The system must be easy to use for customers with no technical experience.
10. All buttons, labels, and instructions must be clearly readable.
11. The booking flow must be completable without any instructions or guidance.
12. The color contrast must meet basic readability standards for all users.
13. The staff dashboard must only be accessible after a valid staff login.
14. The admin panel must only be accessible after valid admin credentials.
15. No customer must be able to access the staff or admin pages directly.
16. All passwords must be stored securely in the database and never in plain text.
17. The system must protect customer personal data from unauthorized access.
18. The system code must be organized across separate HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and PHP files.
19. Each file must handle only its own responsibility to make future updates easy.
20. The system must be easy to extend with new features without rewriting existing code.
21. The support ticket system must notify the admin when a new ticket is submitted.

b. Acceptance Criteria

NFR1 — Page Load Speed
- All four pages load in under 2 seconds on a standard connection
- The booking confirmation screen appears in under 1 second after submitting
- The calendar renders instantly when the booking page is opened
- No page shows a blank screen for more than 1 second

NFR6 — Data Persistence
- All bookings are saved permanently in the MySQL database and do not disappear after closing the browser
- A booking made by one user is visible to the staff and admin immediately after it is confirmed
- No booking data is lost if the page is refreshed or the browser is closed unexpectedly
- All staff and service data remains intact after restarting the XAMPP server

NFR9 — Usability
- A first-time user can complete a booking without any instructions
- The 4-step progress indicator is always visible during the booking flow
- All form fields have clear placeholder text explaining what to enter
- The support ticket form is simple and takes less than 1 minute to fill in

NFR13 — Staff Access Security
- The Staff Dashboard page shows only the login screen before authentication
- Entering a wrong Employee ID or password shows an error message
- The dashboard data is only visible after successful login
- Staff cannot navigate to the admin panel from the staff portal

NFR18 — Code Maintainability
- Each page has its own separate HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and PHP file
- No page's JavaScript file contains logic belonging to another page
- A new developer can understand the file structure within 10 minutes
- Adding a new feature does not require changes to unrelated files


5. Application Specifications

a. Architecture

QueueBank MS follows a three-tier architecture. The Presentation Layer consists of four HTML pages
— Home, User Portal, Staff Dashboard, and Admin Panel — each styled with CSS. The Logic Layer consists
of JavaScript files that handle all interactivity including the booking flow, calendar, form validation,
and data management, along with PHP files that process requests and communicate with the database. The Data 
Layer uses a MySQL database running on XAMPP to store all booking, staff, service, and support ticket data
permanently and consistently across all users and devices.

b. Database Model

QueueBank MS uses MySQL as its data store organized into four tables.

Bookings — stores confirmation code, service name and duration, date and time slot, customer name, email, 
phone, notes, booking status, and created timestamp. Each booking code is unique and each time slot can 
only be booked once per date.

Staff — stores Employee ID, name, email, role, and status. Each Employee ID must be unique. The role field 
stores one of four values: Teller, Senior Teller, Loans Officer, or Supervisor.

Services — stores service ID, icon, name, description, and duration in minutes. All bookings reference
a valid service ID.

Support Tickets — stores ticket ID, customer name, email, subject, message, status (Open or Resolved), and 
created timestamp. Each ticket is linked to a customer email.

c. Technologies Used

- HTML5 — page structure, works in all browsers without installation
- CSS3 — styling and responsive design, full control over the pastel green theme
- JavaScript — all interactivity and booking logic, runs directly in the browser
- PHP — server-side language that connects the website to the MySQL database
- MySQL — relational database that stores all bookings, staff, services, and support tickets permanently
- XAMPP — local server environment that runs Apache, PHP, and MySQL together
- Google Fonts — Playfair Display and Nunito for professional typography
- SVG — custom illustrations, scalable with no external image files needed

d. User Interface Design

The interface uses a pastel green color palette with sage green, mint, teal, and cream as the main colors.
Typography uses Playfair Display for headings and Nunito for body text.

Home Page — public landing page with a hero section featuring a custom illustrated bank scene showing a 
teller serving a customer, two people waiting on a bench, a wall clock, and a calendar. Includes a stats 
bar, a How It Works section with four illustrated steps, and portal selection cards for Customer, Staff, and Admin.

User Portal — two-tab layout with Book Appointment and My Bookings. The booking flow uses a four-step wizard with
a visible step indicator: choose a service, pick a date and time slot, enter personal details, and view the 
confirmation screen with a unique code. Customers can also submit a support ticket from their portal.

Staff Dashboard — protected by a login screen. After login the page shows KPI cards, today's appointment stats, and
a full appointment table with search, filter, and action buttons.

Admin Panel — protected by a separate login with a sidebar navigation. Sections include Dashboard with charts, 
All Bookings, Staff Management with roles, User Records, Services Management, Support Tickets, and System Settings.

e. Security Measures

- Role-based access control — three separate portals for Customer, Staff, and Admin each with different permissions
- Staff authentication — Staff Dashboard requires a valid Employee ID and password before any data is visible
- Admin authentication — Admin Panel uses separate credentials fully independent from staff login
- Input validation — all booking and support ticket forms validate required fields and email format before saving
- Data isolation — customer data is stored securely in the MySQL database on the local server and is only accessible
through the system's PHP files, never exposed directly to the browser or external servers
- Password protection — all passwords are stored securely in the database and never saved in plain text
